## DESCRIPTION
## Percentage of remaining votes to reach parity after some percent has been cast. 
## ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Arithmetic)
## DBchapter(Percents)
## DBsection(Applications)
## KEYWORDS('voting', 'percentage')
## Level(3)
## Static(1)
## MO(1)
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$counted   = random(55, 75, 5);
$remaining = 100 - $counted;
$A         = random(55, 75, 5);
$B         = 100 - $A;

$ans =
    Compute(100 * (0.5 - $B * $counted / 10000) / ($remaining / 100))->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML
In an election, everyone votes for either candidate A or candidate B.

After [$counted]% of the votes are counted, candidate A is leading [$A]% to [$B]%.   
That means that B must get [_]{$ans}% of the remaining vote to have the same number of votes as A.

END_PGML
ANS(Real(65)->cmp);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Suppose that the election has [`T`] voters.  

- After [$counted]% of the votes are counted, [`[$counted/100]\times T`] votes have been counted.  
- A has [$A]% of these votes, that is [`[$A/100]\times [$counted/100]\times T =[$A*$counted/10000]\times T`] votes.  
- B has [$B]% of these votes, that is [`[$B/100]\times [$counted/100]\times T =[$B*$counted/10000]\times T`] votes.  
- Now of the [`[$remaining/100]\times T`]  votes left to be counted, B has to get [`(0.5-[$B*$counted/10000])\times T = [@0.5-$B*$counted/10000@] \times T`] more votes in order to tie A.   

So we have to figure out what percentage of [`[$remaining/100]\times T`] is [`[@0.5-$B*$counted/10000@]\times T`].  This is found with 
[``` \frac{[@0.5-$B*$counted/10000@]}{[$remaining/100]}= [$ans/100]. ```]

So B needs [$ans]% of the remaining vote.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
